            { text: "But the height of the solid EHQP is identical with the height of the pyramid {DEFH|pDEFH},",
              highlighted: BOX2.concat(["pDEFH"]) },
            { text: "and the height of the solid BGML is identical with the height of the pyramid {ABCG|pABCG},",
              highlighted: BOX1.concat(["pABCG"]) },
            { text: "therefore the base {ABC} is to the base {DEF}",
              highlighted: ["ABC","DEF"] },
            { text: "as the height of the pyramid {DEFH|pDEFH} is to the height of the pyramid {ABCG|pABCG}.",
              highlighted: PYRS },
            { text: "Therefore in the pyramids {ABCG|pABCG} and {DEFH|pDEFH} the bases are reciprocally proportional to the heights.",
              highlighted: PYRS.concat(["ABC","DEF"]) },
            // The converse: the pyramids are set out alone again.
            { text: "Next, in the pyramids {ABCG|pABCG} and {DEFH|pDEFH} let the bases be reciprocally proportional to the heights,",
              visible: PYR,
              highlighted: PYRS },
            { text: "that is, as the base {ABC} is to the base {DEF},",
              visible: PYR,
              highlighted: ["ABC","DEF"] },
            { text: "so let the height of the pyramid {DEFH|pDEFH} be to the height of the pyramid {ABCG|pABCG}.",
              visible: PYR,
              highlighted: PYRS },
            { text: "I say that the pyramid {ABCG|pABCG} equals the pyramid {DEFH|pDEFH}.",
              visible: PYR,
              highlighted: PYRS },
            { text: "With the same construction, since the base {ABC} is to the base {DEF}",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["ABC","DEF"] },
            { text: "as the height of the pyramid {DEFH|pDEFH} is to the height of the pyramid {ABCG|pABCG},",
              highlighted: PYRS },
            { text: "while the base {ABC} is to the base {DEF} as the parallelogram {BM} is to the parallelogram {EQ},",
              highlighted: ["ABC","DEF","BM","EQ"] },
            { text: "therefore the parallelogram {BM} is to the parallelogram {EQ}",
              highlighted: ["BM","EQ"] },
            { text: "as the height of the pyramid {DEFH|pDEFH} is to the height of the pyramid {ABCG|pABCG}.",
              highlighted: PYRS,
              justifications: [{ claim: "ratios the same as the same ratio", ref: "V.11" }] },
            { text: "But the height of the pyramid {DEFH|pDEFH} is identical with the height of the parallelepiped EHQP,",
              highlighted: BOX2.concat(["pDEFH"]) },
            { text: "and the height of the pyramid {ABCG|pABCG} is identical with the height of the parallelepiped BGML,",
              highlighted: BOX1.concat(["pABCG"]) },
            { text: "therefore the base {BM} is to the base {EQ}",
              highlighted: ["BM","EQ"] },
            { text: "as the height of the parallelepiped EHQP is to the height of the parallelepiped BGML.",
              highlighted: BOXES },
            { text: "But those parallelepipedal solids in which the bases are reciprocally proportional to the heights are equal,",
              highlighted: BOXES },
            { text: "therefore the parallelepipedal solid BGML equals the parallelepipedal solid EHQP.",
              highlighted: BOXES,
              justifications: [{ claim: "bases reciprocal to heights: solids equal", ref: "XI.34" }] },
            { text: "And the pyramid {ABCG|pABCG} is a sixth part of BGML,",
              highlighted: BOX1.concat(["pABCG"]) },
            { text: "and the pyramid {DEFH|pDEFH} a sixth part of the parallelepiped EHQP,",
              highlighted: BOX2.concat(["pDEFH"]) },
            { text: "therefore the pyramid {ABCG|pABCG} equals the pyramid {DEFH|pDEFH}.",
              highlighted: PYRS },
            { text: "Therefore, in equal pyramids with triangular bases the bases are reciprocally proportional to the heights;",
              highlighted: PYRS.concat(["ABC","DEF"]) },
            { text: "and those pyramids are equal in which the bases are reciprocally proportional to the heights.",
              highlighted: PYRS.concat(["ABC","DEF"]),
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.D." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            if (ref === "XII.9") return "#prop";
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });

    // Per-page citation lookup for the slideshow's resolveJustification.
    window.eucrefs = (window.eucrefs || {});
    Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
        "I.34":      "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI34/",
        "V.11":      "/elements/books/bookV/propositions/propV11/",
        "XI.34":     "/elements/books/bookXI/propositions/propXI34/",
        "XII.7.Cor": "/elements/books/bookXII/propositions/propXII7/#cor"
    });
</script>
</figure>

Now, since the pyramid {ABCG|pABCG} equals the pyramid {DEFH|pDEFH}, and the solid *BGML* is six times the pyramid {ABCG|pABCG}, and the solid *EHQP* six times the pyramid {DEFH|pDEFH}, therefore the solid *BGML* equals the solid *EHQP.* [!just XII.7.Cor]

But in equal parallelepipedal solids the bases are reciprocally proportional to the heights, therefore the base {BM} is to the base {EQ} as the height of the solid *EHQP* is to the height of the solid *BGML.* [!just XI.34]

But the base {BM} is to {EQ} as the triangle {ABC} is to the triangle {DEF}. Therefore the triangle {ABC} is to the triangle {DEF} as the height of the solid *EHQP* is to the height of the solid *BGML.* [!just I.34; V.11]

But the height of the solid *EHQP* is identical with the height of the pyramid {DEFH|pDEFH}, and the height of the solid *BGML* is identical with the height of the pyramid {ABCG|pABCG}, therefore the base {ABC} is to the base {DEF} as the height of the pyramid {DEFH|pDEFH} is to the height of the pyramid {ABCG|pABCG}.

Therefore in the pyramids {ABCG|pABCG} and {DEFH|pDEFH} the bases are reciprocally proportional to the heights.

<hr width="50%">

Next, in the pyramids {ABCG|pABCG} and {DEFH|pDEFH} let the bases be reciprocally proportional to the heights, that is, as the base {ABC} is to the base {DEF}, so let the height of the pyramid {DEFH|pDEFH} be to the height of the pyramid {ABCG|pABCG}.

I say that the pyramid {ABCG|pABCG} equals the pyramid {DEFH|pDEFH}.

With the same construction, since the base {ABC} is to the base {DEF} as the height of the pyramid {DEFH|pDEFH} is to the height of the pyramid {ABCG|pABCG}, while the base {ABC} is to the base {DEF} as the parallelogram {BM} is to the parallelogram {EQ}, therefore the parallelogram {BM} is to the parallelogram {EQ} as the height of the pyramid {DEFH|pDEFH} is to the height of the pyramid {ABCG|pABCG}. [!just V.11]

But the height of the pyramid {DEFH|pDEFH} is identical with the height of the parallelepiped *EHQP,* and the height of the pyramid {ABCG|pABCG} is identical with the height of the parallelepiped *BGML,* therefore the base {BM} is to the base {EQ} as the height of the parallelepiped *EHQP* is to the height of the parallelepiped *BGML.*

But those parallelepipedal solids in which the bases are reciprocally proportional to the heights are equal, therefore the parallelepipedal solid *BGML* equals the parallelepipedal solid *EHQP.* [!just XI.34]

And the pyramid {ABCG|pABCG} is a sixth part of *BGML,* and the pyramid {DEFH|pDEFH} a sixth part of the parallelepiped *EHQP,* therefore the pyramid {ABCG|pABCG} equals the pyramid {DEFH|pDEFH}.

Therefore, *in equal pyramids with triangular bases the bases are reciprocally proportional to the heights; and those pyramids are equal in which the bases are reciprocally proportional to the heights.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
<br clear="all">
----
guide:

The pyramids with triangular bases are one-third of the prisms with triangular bases, which aren't drawn, and the prisms are half of the parallelepipeds. Since the analogous proposition @XI.34 holds for parallelepipeds, this proposition holds for pyramids.

When a similar situation appears later where cones are one-third of cylinders, Euclid simply says the same holds for cones, too, with no details whatsoever.

This proposition completes the theory of volumes for pyramids. The next few propositions treat the theory of volumes of cones and cylinders.
